# Commands on the TEE node (/dev/smcinvoke, tee_device). Its four users,
# qseecomd (tee), KeyMint, Gatekeeper and the fingerprint HAL, send two
# commands to the node: invoke on the root object (0x9801) and create a
# callback server (0x9803). The driver has three more: accept (0x9802) and
# acknowledge (0x9804), which belong to server descriptors, and a log call
# (0x98ff) that none of the four sends.
#
# These rules limit the opened node only. SELinux does not check commands on
# the object and server descriptors that the driver hands out.
#
# They grant nothing by themselves. The ioctl permission comes from the
# platform policy and qva-common/qseecomd.te (tee), from
# vendor-common/hal_keymint_qti.te, from the platform hal_gatekeeper rules and
# from fingerprint.te.
allowxperm tee tee_device:chr_file ioctl { 0x9801 0x9803 };
allowxperm vendor_hal_keymint_qti tee_device:chr_file ioctl { 0x9801 0x9803 };
allowxperm vendor_hal_gatekeeper_qti tee_device:chr_file ioctl { 0x9801 0x9803 };
allowxperm hal_fingerprint_default tee_device:chr_file ioctl { 0x9801 0x9803 };
